        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 经过",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "经过 — jīngguò — \"the sequence of events; what happened\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "他说了那天的经过 tā shuō le nà tiān de jīngguò — He told us what happened that day."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 经 (jīng) — eight strokes in three pieces: the silk piece of 给 on the left, three strokes; on the right a fork of two strokes, a bar that turns into a sweep to the lower left and a short stroke falling to the right, over 工, three strokes. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "经过 (jīngguò) is built from 经 (jīng) 'to pass through' and 过 'to go past', both inherited from Middle Chinese, keng and kwaH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'the warp of a cloth' and 'a path' and the second 'to cross' and 'to go beyond'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling what happened."
